# Exercise: Accessors
#
# Once you can build a `Cons` cell with two fields, you need a way
# to pull those fields back out. The primitive is:
#
#   variant.field-at  ( Variant Int -- Field )
#       returns the field at the given 0-based index.
#
# `prepend` stored head at index 0 and tail at index 1 (the order
# they sat on the stack when `variant.make-2` ran), so reading them
# back is a single `field-at` call each.
#
# Lisp lineage: `head` is `car`, `tail` is `cdr`. Same operations.
#
# Your task: Define
#
#   head  ( List -- T )      — first element of a non-empty list
#   tail  ( List -- List )   — the rest of the list
#
# Calling `head` or `tail` on an empty list is undefined — a real
# library would either pre-check with `empty?` or return a Result.
# Here we just trust the caller.
#
# When done, delete the marker line below.

# I AM NOT DONE

# ─── Already defined for you ───────────────────────────────────
: empty ( -- Variant )         :Empty variant.make-0 ;
: prepend ( T Variant -- Variant ) :Cons variant.make-2 ;
: empty? ( Variant -- Bool )    variant.tag :Empty symbol.= ;
# ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

: head ( Variant -- T )
    # Your code here — pull field 0 with variant.field-at.
    drop 0
;

: tail ( Variant -- Variant )
    # Your code here — pull field 1 with variant.field-at.
    drop empty
;

# Do not edit below this line

: test-head ( -- )
    empty 3 swap prepend 2 swap prepend 1 swap prepend
    head 1 test.assert-eq
;

: test-tail-head ( -- )
    # tail of (1 2 3) is (2 3); head of that is 2
    empty 3 swap prepend 2 swap prepend 1 swap prepend
    tail head 2 test.assert-eq
;
